DatA CRASHES THE COMPILER AND NOTHING ELSE IN ITS FAMILY DOES

The genParse count harness (genLadder/countPop.sh) attempts all 42 installed
rules one process at a time. On 2026-08-24 it reported 22 compiled clean, 19
parse-failed, and exactly one CRASH. The crash is DatA, signal 11.

READ THE OUTPUT LIKE THIS. This file is the rare fixit whose SENTINEL IS THE
REMEDY SIGNAL. While the defect is present the run dies at C1 and prints
neither C2 nor the sentinel, so a foot that is never reached is the
demonstration rather than a truncation to worry about. When the defect is
fixed, C2 and the sentinel both appear. Row 0 is the anti-vacuity control --
it proves the subject exists and is non-empty before anything is attempted,
so a silent run cannot be mistaken for a missing rule.

WHAT IS KNOWN

The frame, captured under `script -q /dev/null` (bear-trap: a segfault loses
buffered stdout, so the pty is not optional):

    Bad pointer dereference at 0x0000000000000030
    0  GroupItem::addAttribute(GroupItem*)   GroupItem.mm:199:28
    1  compile                               GroupRules.mm:2617:10
    2  runOP                                 GroupRules.mm:12576:12
    3  aCTionBlocK                           GroupRules.mm:85:13

DatA is a container rule -- a bare name followed by an indented list of
alternatives, with no terms of its own:

    DatA
        GrouP;
        NumbeR;
        ANYtoken=NamE;
        ANYstring=[^ \n\r\t;]+;

⚠ AND THE COMPARISON THAT MAKES IT INTERESTING: WardeD IS THE SAME SHAPE AND
COMPILES CLEAN. WardeD is also a bare container name over an indented
alternative list, it is in the same 42, and it is one of the 22 that pass. So
"container rule" is not by itself the trigger, and any theory that stops there
is already refuted by a rule sitting in the same run.

WHAT IS NOT KNOWN, AND THE WARNING THAT GOES WITH THE FRAME

⚠ BEAR-TRAP 36 APPLIES AND IT IS THE FIRST THING TO GET WRONG HERE. A
backtrace names the line that DIED, not the line that read null. Frame 1 says
GroupRules.mm:2617, which is where compile called addAttribute; the null it
handed over may have been produced several lines earlier, and GroupItem.mm:199
is the CONSUMER of a bad `this` or argument, not its source. A control written
against 2617 inherits that off-by-one. Walk backward to the fetch before
writing either number down as a target.

Nothing here is diagnosed. The rule was classified as an ORPHAN in the
2026-08-24 family triage -- families A, B and C each have a mechanism and a
control, and DatA belongs to none of them.

